INT. MATTRESS SHOWROOM - SATURDAY AFTERNOON

A cavernous store. Hundreds of mattresses in neat rows under blazing lights. A banner hangs from the rafters: EVERYTHING MUST GO. Soft muzak plays. The floor is carpeted a shade of regret.

HARLAN FEENEY (50s, salesman, tan suit, a name tag that says HARLAN - ASK ME ABOUT SLEEP) glides between the beds with a grin. DOT BELLAMY (70s, a tiny woman in a quilted jacket, huge handbag, orthopedic sneakers) stalks the aisles, pressing each mattress with her palm. WALLACE BELLAMY (70s, her husband, suspenders, a newspaper rolled in his fist) trails behind her at a respectful distance.

HARLAN
Welcome! First time in the market for a mattress?

DOT
Forty-one years.

HARLAN
Wonderful. Then you know the value of a good night's sleep.

DOT
I know the value of a good price.

She climbs onto a bed in her sneakers. Harlan's smile locks in place. She lies flat, hands folded across her handbag. Wallace perches on the edge of the next bed and opens his newspaper.

DOT (CONT'D)
Too soft.

HARLAN
Ma'am, shoes.

DOT
Wallace, test this.

WALLACE
I'm reading.

DOT
Test it.

Wallace sighs, folds the paper, and lies down on the adjacent mattress with his shoes on. Harlan winces visibly. Both Bellamys lie side by side on separate beds, staring at the ceiling, like patients in a ward.

WALLACE
It's all right.

DOT
It's not all right.

WALLACE
It's fine.

DOT
You said that in 1983, and we had to buy the one with the lump.

HARLAN
This is our Cloud Supreme. Eight inches of memory foam, with a ten-year warranty.

DOT
Memory of what?

HARLAN
Of you, ma'am. It remembers your shape.

DOT
I don't want it to remember me. I want it to forget.

HARLAN
(beat)
That's, uh, our Nap Plus model.

DOT
How much?

HARLAN
Fourteen hundred.

DOT
For a bed.

HARLAN
For a sleep.

Dot sits up, swings her legs over the side, and looks at him for a long, hard moment. Then she slaps her palm down on the mattress.

DOT
Eight hundred, and you throw in the pillows.

HARLAN
Ma'am, I can't--

DOT
Wallace. Get up. We're leaving.

WALLACE
I'm comfortable.

DOT
Wallace!

He rises slowly, unwillingly, shoes squeaking. Harlan raises both hands.

HARLAN
Twelve hundred. And the pillows.

DOT
One thousand.

HARLAN
Eleven.

DOT
One thousand, and we take the floor model, and you deliver it Tuesday.

HARLAN
(defeated)
Done.

DOT
Wallace, pay the man.

Wallace pulls out his wallet. A moth flutters out. Harlan stares at it, stares at Dot, and takes the wallet gently, as if it might bite.
